}

\p{All 226 declines are the eager-reduction rule (\code{\verb~|E_LIMIT~}, \code{\verb~|eagerDeclines~}), none is an audit failure. On these files every T of a shortcut whose re-derivation completed is also a T of the complete official procedure (489,860 audits; 15,632 of them = 3.2 \% ran out of the audit budget of 65,536 ticks and are NOT confirmed): the masks never turned an official F into a T. (The control: the same script on S's \code{\verb~|facts-agreement-noncongruent-app-bad~} reports the two \code{\verb~|E_AUDIT~} declines it should.) This is evidence for the masks on real signatures, not a proof: a wrong mask bit on a position whose arguments never differ in these files would not show.}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.7 Mutants: a Mathlib slice and the official \code{\verb~|bugs~} corpus}

\p{The official mutant corpus \code{\verb~|work/mutants-bugs-other~} (mutants of the arena's \code{\verb~|bugs~}, \code{\verb~|other~}, \code{\verb~|corner-cases~} tests, 22 kinds, classified by the official checker): the 2,040 mutants that the official KERNEL rejects, under \code{\verb~|skip~} and \code{\verb~|both~} (\code{\verb~|work/s2/out/mutants-bugs-judged.tsv~}): \strong{1,977 REJECT, 63 DECLINE (declines of the pipeline: never an accept), 0 ACCEPT.}}

\p{\strong{Targeted mutants of a Mathlib slice} (\code{\verb~|work/s2/gen/mutate.py~} = S's mutator restricted to the kinds \code{\verb~|SIBLING_ARG~}, \code{\verb~|LIT_SUCC_NE~}, \code{\verb~|LEVEL_ARG~}):}

\p{360 mutants of \code{\verb~|Mathlib.Algebra.Group.Basic.cone~} (1,724 units; each mutant edits ONE occurrence inside ONE declaration: the argument of an application replaced by the argument of another application of the same function, a Nat literal \code{\verb~|n~} by \code{\verb~|Nat.succ n~}, one universe argument of a constant by another level; 174 in a declared type, 186 in a value). \code{\verb~|work/s2/out/mut-mathlib-judged.tsv~}; the 360 files (1.9 GB) were deleted after the run, their descriptions and SHA-256 sums are in \code{\verb~|work/s2/attacks/mut-mathlib/~} and the command of section 7 regenerates them:}

\table{
\tr{\th{kind}\th{mutants}\th{official reject}\th{of them rejected under \code{\verb~|skip~} and \code{\verb~|both~}}\th{official accept (accepted under both)}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|SIBLING_ARG~}}\td{120}\td{118}\td{\strong{118}}\td{2}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|LIT_SUCC_NE~}}\td{120}\td{120}\td{\strong{120}}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|LEVEL_ARG~}}\td{120}\td{120}\td{\strong{120}}\td{0}}
}

\p{In a sample of 12 of them the machine's rejection is \code{\verb~|E_APP_MISMATCH~} (the type of an argument against its domain: a comparison) in 9 and a declaration-level type mismatch in 1 more: these mutants are decided by comparisons of the kind the options shorten, on Mathlib's own signatures (instance arguments, proof fields).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{4. The known exports that the options accept and the official checker rejects}

\p{Notation: \code{\verb~|I = And.intro True True True.intro True.intro~}; \code{\verb~|h~}, \code{\verb~|h2~} axioms of \code{\verb~|And True True~}; \code{\verb~|R x = And.rec (motive := fun _ => Nat) (fun _ _ => 5) x~}; PI = proof irrelevance; "cong" = congruence of application / of substitution. Verdicts of this review's run (jar \code{\verb~|54eeb7cc50dd~}, \code{\verb~|work/s2/out/known-judged.tsv~}): A = accepted, R = rejected; the official checker rejects every row.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Export}\th{Claim}\th{\code{\verb~|skip~}}\th{\code{\verb~|cong~}}\th{\code{\verb~|both~}}\th{Derivation in the theory}\th{Class}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|probe-b1-rec-proof-major-bad~}, \code{\verb~|-flipped-bad~} (S-F1; pinned by \code{\verb~|M4OfficialTest~})}\td{\code{\verb~|R I = R h~}}\td{R}\td{A}\td{A}\td{\code{\verb~|h = I~} by PI (both inhabit the proposition \code{\verb~|And True True~}); cong under \code{\verb~|And.rec .. _~}. (Then \code{\verb~|R I = 5~} by iota, beta: the official procedure reduces the left side and cannot move the right)}\td{TRUE; official incompleteness}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|probe-b12-closure-proof-slot-bad~} (S-F1; pinned)}\td{\code{\verb~|(fun x => g (R x)) I = (fun x => g (R x)) h~}}\td{R}\td{A}\td{A}\td{beta on both sides, then the row above under \code{\verb~|g~}}\td{TRUE}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|probe-b12-let-proof-slot-bad~}}\td{\code{\verb~|(let x := I; g (R x)) = (let x := h; g (R x))~}}\td{R}\td{A}\td{A}\td{zeta, then as above}\td{TRUE}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|probe-b12-lam-closure-proof-slot-bad~}}\td{\code{\verb~|(fun x => g (fun n => R x)) I = (..) h~}}\td{R}\td{A}\td{A}\td{beta; cong under the lambda (xi) and \code{\verb~|g~}; \code{\verb~|R I = R h~}}\td{TRUE}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|probe-b1-abbrev-proof-arg~}}\td{\code{\verb~|F I = F h~}, \code{\verb~|F := fun x => R x~} an abbrev}\td{R}\td{A}\td{A}\td{\code{\verb~|I = h~} by PI, cong under \code{\verb~|F~} (or delta on both sides and the first row). Officially an abbrev head is unfolded without comparing arguments}\td{TRUE}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|probe-b1-theorem-head~}}\td{the same, \code{\verb~|F~} with hint opaque}\td{R}\td{A}\td{A}\td{the same}\td{TRUE}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|probe-b1-rec-under-ctor-bad~}}\td{\code{\verb~|Nat.succ (R I) = Nat.succ (R h)~}}\td{R}\td{A}\td{A}\td{the first row under \code{\verb~|Nat.succ~}}\td{TRUE}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|probe-b1-rec-under-axiom-bad~}}\td{\code{\verb~|g (R I) = g (R h)~}}\td{R}\td{A}\td{A}\td{the first row under \code{\verb~|g~}}\td{TRUE}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|facts-agreement-noncongruent-app-bad~} (S-F2; pinned)}\td{\code{\verb~|w I p1 = w h p2~}, \code{\verb~|w : (x : And True True) -> Q (R x) -> Nat~}, \code{\verb~|p1 : Q (R I)~}, \code{\verb~|p2 : Q (R h)~}}\td{A}\td{A}\td{A}\td{\code{\verb~|I = h~} by PI; so \code{\verb~|Q (R I) = Q (R h)~} by cong; \code{\verb~|p1~}, \code{\verb~|p2~} are then proofs of one proposition: \code{\verb~|p1 = p2~} by PI; cong under \code{\verb~|w~}}\td{TRUE}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|facts-agreement-noncongruent-samedef-bad~}}\td{the same, \code{\verb~|w~} a regular definition}\td{A}\td{A}\td{A}\td{the same (or delta first)}\td{TRUE}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|facts-etas-prop-mask-noncongruent-bad~}}\td{\code{\verb~|S.mk I p1 s.3 = s~}, \code{\verb~|s~} an axiom of the structure}\td{A}\td{A}\td{A}\td{structure eta: \code{\verb~|s = S.mk s.1 s.2 s.3~}; \code{\verb~|s.1 = I~} by PI; \code{\verb~|s.2 : Q (R s.1) = Q (R I)~}, so \code{\verb~|s.2 = p1~} by PI; cong under \code{\verb~|S.mk~}}\td{TRUE}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|facts-unread-noncongruent-bad~}}\td{\code{\verb~|d 0 I = d 1 h~}, \code{\verb~|d u x := R x~}}\td{A}\td{R}\td{R}\td{delta, beta: \code{\verb~|d 0 I = R I~}, \code{\verb~|d 1 h = R h~}; the first row. (\code{\verb~|both~} rejects only because the quick refutation of pass A answers F on \code{\verb~|0~} / \code{\verb~|1~} before the plan; the equation is the same)}\td{TRUE}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|open-flag-closed-bad~} (package S, batch 8; NOT in the list of S-F1 / S-F2, it was written after M4's fix)}\td{\code{\verb~|Nat.add (G I) 0 = Nat.add (G h) 0~}, \code{\verb~|G x := And.rec (fun _ _ => 0) x~} regular, both sides closed}\td{R}\td{A}\td{A}\td{\code{\verb~|I = h~} by PI, cong under \code{\verb~|G~} and \code{\verb~|Nat.add _ 0~}. Officially both sides are closed, so the left side is folded to \code{\verb~|0~} before any same-head comparison, and \code{\verb~|0~} against the stuck right side is false; B1 compares the arguments of the two \code{\verb~|Nat.add~} first}\td{TRUE}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|acc-rec-intro-vs-axiom~} (new, \code{\verb~|work/s2/attacks2/~})}\td{\code{\verb~|Acc.rec F (Acc.intro true k) = Acc.rec F h~}, \code{\verb~|h~} an axiom of \code{\verb~|Acc R true~}}\td{R}\td{A}\td{A}\td{\code{\verb~|h = Acc.intro true k~} by PI; cong under \code{\verb~|Acc.rec F _~}}\td{TRUE}}
}

\p{\strong{The classification is checked by the official checker itself} (\code{\verb~|work/s2/gen/certify.py~}, \code{\verb~|work/s2/certify/~}, 15 exports, one per row above, both directions of the first): each proves the SAME statement not by \code{\verb~|Eq.refl~} but by the derivation of the table written as a term - \code{\verb~|Eq.rec~} (congruence) over \code{\verb~|Eq.refl x : x = y~} for the two proofs \code{\verb~|x~}, \code{\verb~|y~}, an equation the official checker accepts by proof irrelevance; for the agreement and structure eta rows the motive quantifies over the dependent proof (\code{\verb~|fun x _ => forall (p : Q (R x)), w I p1 = w x p~}). \strong{The official checker accepts all 15} (so do the machine under \code{\verb~|v0~}, \code{\verb~|rich~}, \code{\verb~|both~} and both leaders). What this certifies: every statement that the options accept against the official verdict is a THEOREM for the official checker, so its acceptance is not the acceptance of a false statement. That the equation is moreover DEFINITIONAL in the theory is the derivation of the table: proof irrelevance and congruence are conversion rules of the theory, the certificate uses nothing else (\code{\verb~|Eq.rec~} on \code{\verb~|Eq.refl~} is how one writes them as a term). The official \code{\verb~|is_def_eq~} fails to FIND these equations - it reduces one side before it compares, and it does not compare the arguments of an abbrev, a recursor or a closure -; none of them is false.}

\p{The whole run (\code{\verb~|work/s2/out/known-judged.tsv~}, configurations \code{\verb~|v0~}, \code{\verb~|rich~}, \code{\verb~|skip~}, \code{\verb~|cong~}, \code{\verb~|both~}, \code{\verb~|bothrich~}):}

\table{
\tr{\th{Set}\th{exports}\th{official reject}\th{of them accepted under an option}\th{rejected under every configuration}\th{official accept (accepted under every configuration)}}
\tr{\td{package S (\code{\verb~|work/soundness/exports/~})}\td{351}\td{304}\td{\strong{14} (the rows above without the last)}\td{289 (+ 1 declined by the pipeline in every configuration, \code{\verb~|str-lit-missing-prerequisites-bad~})}\td{47}}
\tr{\td{the arena's \code{\verb~|bad~} tests (\code{\verb~|work/arena-site/tests/bad/~}: 32 bugs, 5 other, 8 perf, 47 tutorial)}\td{92}\td{92}\td{\strong{0}}\td{\strong{92}}\td{-}}
}

\p{No export is accepted under \code{\verb~|v0~} or \code{\verb~|rich~} that the official checker rejects.}

\p{Rejected by the options although TRUE in the theory (so the options are not complete for the theory either, and do not need to be): \code{\verb~|nontransitive-regular-def-bad~}, \code{\verb~|nontransitive-same-decl-bad~} (\code{\verb~|d h = 5~}: delta, the first row, iota; nothing in the machine closes pairs transitively), the arena's \code{\verb~|125_accRecNoEta~} and \code{\verb~|101_ruleKAcc~} (\code{\verb~|Acc.rec (fun _ _ _ => p) h = p~} for a variable \code{\verb~|h~}: TRUE by PI against \code{\verb~|Acc.intro~} + iota; the arena lists them as \code{\verb~|bad~}; no shortcut synthesises a constructor form from a proof, every configuration rejects), and \code{\verb~|acc-rec-no-eta-axiom~} of this review.}

\p{Not in this class (false or not derivable; rejected by every configuration): the other 289 + 92 officially rejected exports of package S and of the arena, among them the eager-reduction rows (declined by the machine by rule M6-F26, rejected by the reference engine) and \code{\verb~|100_ruleKbad~} (\code{\verb~|Eq.rec a (h : true = false) = a~}: not derivable, \code{\verb~|h~} is no \code{\verb~|Eq.refl~}).}
}

\subtree{
\title{5. The leaders}

\p{Read: \code{\verb~|refs/gruszka/src/tc.rs~} (\code{\verb~|spines_defeq_skip~} 8573, \code{\verb~|unused_args~} 8603, \code{\verb~|prop_positions~} / \code{\verb~|surely_prop~} 8641-8721, \code{\verb~|defeq_app~} 8770, \code{\verb~|try_eta_struct~} 8823, \code{\verb~|closure_congr~} 6792, the same-head step of lazy delta 8180, \code{\verb~|race_k~} 7831, \code{\verb~|probe_args~} 8414), \code{\verb~|refs/nano-lean/src/value_checker/conv.rs~} (\code{\verb~|def_eq_core~} 246, \code{\verb~|relevant~} 389, \code{\verb~|prop_type~} 433, \code{\verb~|args_eq~} 455, \code{\verb~|probe_args~} 359, \code{\verb~|def_eq_app~} 587, \code{\verb~|eta_struct~} 628, \code{\verb~|same~} 107). Both are the same family of shortcuts (gruszka names its source: Pestka's ProofArgsSkipped, unusedArgs, EtaProofFieldsSkipped).}

\table{
\tr{\th{}\th{machine (\code{\verb~|skipargs~}, \code{\verb~|congruence~})}\th{gruszka}\th{nano-lean}}
\tr{\td{"position is a proof"}\td{INFER (infer-only) of the domain under the telescope returns a SORT record; level instantiated at the head's levels; exact always-zero test}\td{syntactic: after the domain's own Pis, a variable or constant applied to as many arguments as its type has Pis, that type ending in a sort; level instantiated at the head's levels (\code{\verb~|zeroish~})}\td{syntactic, the same shape; the sort must be always zero AS WRITTEN (zero, \code{\verb~|max~}, \code{\verb~|imax _ 0~}): no instantiation of universe parameters (\code{\verb~|Sort u~} at \code{\verb~|u := 0~} is compared)}}
\tr{\td{proof position whose domain mentions a skipped, different unread position}\td{compared (\code{\verb~|differ~} rule)}\td{skipped}\td{skipped}}
\tr{\td{unread positions}\td{definitions and theorems, spine >= lamArity, superset mask up to 65,535 lambdas, first 64 positions}\td{definitions only (not theorems), at most 64 lambdas, spine >= lambdas}\td{definitions and theorems, also for a spine shorter than the lambdas (the rest of the lambdas counts as body)}}
\tr{\td{where the masks are used}\td{c8 (proof positions), same-head step of lazy delta for a regular definition, B1}\td{\code{\verb~|defeq_app~} (proof positions), same-head step for a regular definition, budgeted probes (\code{\verb~|race_k~}, \code{\verb~|probe_args~}, refutation)}\td{\code{\verb~|def_eq_app~}, \code{\verb~|probe_args~} before \code{\verb~|whnf_core~} for every unfoldable same head (budget)}}
\tr{\td{same-head probe on recursors / \code{\verb~|Quot.lift~}}\td{yes (B1, NOTES-M4 D19)}\td{none in the code I read (it accepts the \code{\verb~|And.rec~} rows of section 4 by a route I did not trace)}\td{no}}
\tr{\td{structure eta: proof fields}\td{skipped}\td{skipped}\td{compared (proof irrelevance)}}
\tr{\td{closure congruence}\td{slots by the complete CONV (B12)}\td{slots by identity and by pairs ALREADY found equal (\code{\verb~|same_val~}), never a comparison of its own}\td{structural identity only (\code{\verb~|same~}, fuel 512)}}
\tr{\td{proofs by type before \code{\verb~|whnf_core~}}\td{B2, when the value is already known to be a proof}\td{no}\td{yes, always (\code{\verb~|proof_irrel~} precedes \code{\verb~|whnf_core~})}}
}

\p{Precautions the machine takes and the leaders do not: the \code{\verb~|differ~} rule; exact level instantiation with a semantic sort (nano-lean is MORE conservative here: no instantiation). Precautions the leaders take and the machine does not: none that bears on soundness. gruszka restricts unread positions to definitions and to 64 lambdas, nano-lean compares proof fields in structure eta and never compares closure slots by conversion: all three are restrictions of completeness / cost, not conditions of the equations. I could not construct a false equation from the absence of the \code{\verb~|differ~} rule (section 2.2: the two proofs then have types that the consumer's side forces to agree, or the pair is never asked), so I do not claim the leaders are unsound there; the machine's rule is the more careful one and costs a mask intersection.}

\p{Run (Docker, no network, the arena's run lines; \code{\verb~|work/s2/leaders.sh~}, \code{\verb~|work/s2/out/leaders.tsv~}):}

\table{
\tr{\th{official}\th{gruszka}\th{nano-lean}\th{exports (872: all of 3.1 with their non-literal twins, 3.2, 3.3, the certificates of section 4, S's conversion groups \code{\verb~|facts-*~}, \code{\verb~|probe-*~}, \code{\verb~|mask-*~}, \code{\verb~|pirr-*~}, \code{\verb~|nontransitive-*~}, \code{\verb~|eta-*~}, \code{\verb~|unitlike-*~}, \code{\verb~|k-eq-rec-*~}, \code{\verb~|krule-*~}, \code{\verb~|open-flag-*~}, the arena's \code{\verb~|100_ruleKbad~}, \code{\verb~|101_ruleKAcc~}, \code{\verb~|125_accRecNoEta~})}}
\tr{\td{accept}\td{accept}\td{accept}\td{253}}
\tr{\td{reject}\td{reject}\td{reject}\td{600}}
\tr{\td{reject}\td{\strong{accept}}\td{\strong{accept}}\td{9}}
\tr{\td{reject}\td{\strong{accept}}\td{reject}\td{10}}
}

\ul{
\li{Neither leader accepts any FALSE claim of this review (the 96 hand-written and 362 random \code{\verb~|-bad~} exports) or of package S.}
\li{\strong{gruszka accepts all 14 exports of package S} that the machine accepts under the options (the 13 of S-F1 / S-F2 and \code{\verb~|open-flag-closed-bad~}); \strong{nano-lean accepts 6 of them} (\code{\verb~|facts-agreement-noncongruent-app-bad~}, \code{\verb~|-samedef-bad~}, \code{\verb~|facts-unread-noncongruent-bad~}, \code{\verb~|probe-b1-abbrev-proof-arg~}, \code{\verb~|probe-b1-theorem-head~}, \code{\verb~|open-flag-closed-bad~}): its probe is for unfoldable heads only and it has no closure congruence and no ETAS mask.}
\li{Both leaders accept three more officially rejected exports that the machine REJECTS also with the options: \code{\verb~|krule-eager-alone-bad~}, \code{\verb~|krule-memo-across-binders-eager-bad~}, \code{\verb~|krule-memo-across-declarations-eager-bad~} (by package S's description the same class, inside the K rule under eager reduction; the machine declines such a declaration, the reference engine rejects it). gruszka also accepts \code{\verb~|nontransitive-regular-def-bad~} and \code{\verb~|nontransitive-same-decl-bad~} (\code{\verb~|d h = 5~}, true in the theory; by which route I did not trace).}
\li{Neither leader accepts \code{\verb~|acc-rec-intro-vs-axiom~} (B1 on a recursor head is the machine's alone; why gruszka accepts the \code{\verb~|And.rec~} rows and not this one I did not trace), nor the arena's \code{\verb~|125_accRecNoEta~}, \code{\verb~|101_ruleKAcc~}, \code{\verb~|100_ruleKbad~}. Both accept the 15 certificates of section 4, as the official checker does.}
}

\p{So "as the leaders do" is literally true for gruszka: on these 872 exports gruszka differs from the official verdict on 19, nano-lean on 9, the machine on 15 under some configuration with an option (14 of package S and the \code{\verb~|Acc~} analogue; 14 of the 15 under \code{\verb~|both~}), on 4 with \code{\verb~|skipargs~} alone, on none without the options.}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. Not established}

\ul{
\li{\strong{No proof.} The verdict rests on reading the code, on the argument of section 2 and on the attacks; nothing is mechanised. The induction of 2.2 uses the standard meta-theory of the official algorithm (subject reduction, uniqueness of types up to conversion, soundness of the official rules), which is itself not mechanised for all of Lean 4.}
\li{\strong{The precondition of 2.1} (every comparison is between well-typed terms) was established by reading the call sites of \code{\verb~|Conv.conv~} / \code{\verb~|Cargs.cargs~} and by the \code{\verb~|ctx~} attacks, not exhaustively. Not read line by line: the K1 code objects (\code{\verb~|Code~}, \code{\verb~|Elab~}, case trees, BODY / RULE / LEAF blocks), \code{\verb~|Rules~} (\code{\verb~|k2~}), \code{\verb~|Tier~} (\code{\verb~|t2~}), \code{\verb~|Same~} (the \code{\verb~|same~} switch; it was ON in \code{\verb~|rich~} / \code{\verb~|bothrich~} / \code{\verb~|bothall~} runs, but it is M4's official-exact claim and S's to attack). They reduce and identify; a fault there is a fault of the machine without the options.}
\li{\strong{Inference is now the only line of defence} for proof arguments and unread arguments (section 0, point 2). How likely a fault of INFER is that the type comparison of proof irrelevance would have caught, I cannot say; one seeded example shows the mechanism, no real one is known.}
\li{\strong{Parallel workers}: the publication of fact records (\code{\verb~|FactStore~}: one compare-and-set per row, immutable records) was reviewed by reading; one configuration with four workers ran the 134 hand-written attacks only. No export targets a race.}
\li{\strong{Native image}: every run is the JVM launcher.}
\li{\strong{Scale}: \code{\verb~|shortcheck~} ran on the slices listed in 3.6, not on all of Mathlib, Std or Init (Init: M4, before D27, NOTES-M4). The whole-Mathlib runs of P8 show that every unit is accepted, which says nothing about soundness.}
\li{\strong{Shapes without a hand-written attack}: mutual and nested inductive types, string literals (\code{\verb~|String~} is a structure with a proof field in this toolchain: the ETAS mask applies to it), \code{\verb~|Nat~} literal views. They occur in the Mathlib slices of 3.6 / 3.7 only.}
\li{\strong{Positions from 64 on} are always compared; nothing was skipped there to attack. Telescopes above 65,535 binders get no facts (not exercised).}
\li{\strong{The leaders} were run on the hostile exports only as black boxes; their sources were read for the shortcuts, not audited.}
\li{\strong{Universe levels} (finding S-F3 of package S, the official incomplete relation against the complete one) are outside this review; every run used the default (official) relation.}
\li{\strong{Eager reduction}: a declaration that enters it is declined by the machine in every configuration, with the options as without; the options were therefore never exercised under ER.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. Reproduction}

\pre{\verb~|
python3 -I work/s2/gen/attacks.py                      # work/s2/attacks/*.ndjson (134) + attacks.tsv
S2_DAT=1 python3 -I work/s2/gen/attacks.py             # work/s2/attacks-dat/ (the same, no literal arguments)
python3 -I work/s2/gen/attacks2.py                     # work/s2/attacks2/ (Acc.rec, Quot, unit-like, Prop structures)
python3 -I work/s2/gen/certify.py                      # work/s2/certify/cert-*.ndjson: section 4 as terms for the official checker
for f in work/s2/certify/cert-*.ndjson; do rlvm-testkit/bin/testkit verdict $f; done     # 15 x accept
python3 -I work/s2/gen/fuzz.py SEED 100 work/s2/attacks/fuzz      # seeds 11..14
python3 -I work/s2/gen/mutate.py work/s2/attacks/mut-mathlib --per 120 --seed 7 work/exports/slices/good/Mathlib.Algebra.Group.Basic.cone.ndjson
scripts/build.sh --published --jvm-only --dist work/s2/dist2
bench/slot.sh -- python3 -I work/s2/judge.py --manifest work/s2/attacks/attacks.tsv --out OUT.tsv -j 3   # one line per export
bench/slot.sh -- work/s2/campaign.sh                   # known exports, leaders (Docker), corpora with shortcheck, mutants, seeded faults
bench/slot.sh -- work/s2/campaign2.sh                  # attacks2, certificates under the machine, leaders on them
python3 -I work/s2/leaders-summary.py                  # official / gruszka / nano-lean on every hostile export
work/s2/stats.sh congruence,skipargs warm,gated,expect,facts,probes FILE     # one run with the shortcut counters
~}
}
